#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157106 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e538c21e-67fd-56f7-b09f-a9fec3121f78" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Graphically"}] :question/id #uuid "af1244b4-53fb-54ed-920b-6054a480b211" :question/math-academy-id "e-7884" :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/8c6c7cf5762a3078063987d1dc5eb95f.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0, \\pm a)$. From the graph, we have that $(0,9)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=9$.\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny = \\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny = \\pm 3 x\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a}{b} &= 3 \\\\\n\\frac{9}{b} &= 3 \\\\\nb &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\tan{t},\\quad y=9\\sec{t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157107 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6e4940d-fc80-563d-85a2-b83478cce6c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/id #uuid "9ddfab47-6101-5777-b4fa-780cc382cc25" :question/math-academy-id "e-7886" :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{y^2}{9} - \\dfrac{x^2}{25} = 1$?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx = b\\tan{t}, \\quad y = a\\sec{t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nb = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx = 5\\tan{t}, \\quad y = 3\\sec{t}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157108 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "494f9793-de3b-5a0e-8ee9-38a8bad0f970" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Hyperbola Given its Parametric Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "01791465-d1dc-5f4a-ac9a-72b822ab08b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7887" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x =2\\tan{t}, \\, y =3\\sec{t}$." :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t$. We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n$x|= 2\\tan t; \\frac{x}{2}|= \\tan t; \\frac{x^{2}}{2^{2}}|= \\tan^{2} t; \\frac{x^{2}}{4}|= \\tan^{2} t$.\n- Isolating the $\\sec{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n$y|= 3\\sec t; \\frac{y}{3}|= \\sec t; \\frac{y^{2}}{3^{2}}|= \\sec^{2} t; \\frac{y^{2}}{9}|= \\sec^{2} t$.\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}\n$$\n\nand rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan^{2} t + 1 &= \\sec^{2} t \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + 1 &= \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{9} - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157124 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/id #uuid "fc3042ef-d85e-54e2-8ea6-cd8570bcdd6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8055" :question/problem "The quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/b31abfb21d7c23e3c4f5c2a1ed1eb344.png)\n\n$x = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad t \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad t \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/ff70cc02658a737d406109329d5c2ccf.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[MATH: \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, 0\\right]]$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$[MATH: x= \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,0\\right].]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6e4940d-fc80-563d-85a2-b83478cce6c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Vertical Hyperbola Given Algebraically"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb5fd77d-d86f-52ca-803c-90632f9c4a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 25\\sec t, y = 16\\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156683} {:answer/id #uuid "dea51195-05a9-5077-9b8f-1cb5210ecbe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 16\\tan t, y = 25\\sec t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156684} {:answer/id #uuid "0756c206-2570-5d2b-a144-a0b47fb8ab2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 5\\tan t, y = 4\\sec t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156685} {:answer/id #uuid "42fbf47a-175f-53a6-83ec-593a67683c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 5\\sec t, y = 4\\tan t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156686} {:answer/id #uuid "45fb2299-8011-52f8-b3d5-997e406f2537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\tan t, y = 5\\sec t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45fb2299-8011-52f8-b3d5-997e406f2537" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x = 4\\tan t, y = 5\\sec t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156687} :answer-field/id #uuid "e604fdc9-7e1f-58f2-89eb-02dfcad4d252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156682}] :question/id #uuid "181cf72e-dc08-50f6-a54a-4c45042abcc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-89068" :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\frac{y^{2}}{25} - \\frac{x^{2}}{16} = 1$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bc9ab26-c20e-5964-b619-80aab04f4e14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [0, π], t \\ne \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156863} {:answer/id #uuid "ca3fd94a-2d9e-5568-a52c-896ace185c94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156864} {:answer/id #uuid "fe611f12-b578-556c-8d83-8582ecacc5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{π}{2})∪(\\frac{π}{2}, π]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156865} {:answer/id #uuid "032f2c49-77a0-53ab-9bba-4be703482db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{3π}{2})∪(\\frac{3π}{2}, π]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156866} {:answer/id #uuid "04b8bccc-5d46-5f5b-b488-e462f900369a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [- π, 0], t \\ne - \\frac{π}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe611f12-b578-556c-8d83-8582ecacc5d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{π}{2})∪(\\frac{π}{2}, π]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156865} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad0c93a8-8a98-56cf-ab1e-f08a00b1e217" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156862}] :question/id #uuid "ce7100e2-18bc-5970-9831-7deb51318ec4" :question/math-academy-id "q-90951" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/q-90951.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\tan t, y = 2\\sec t$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0dfdda62-1838-540f-bf4d-960353fc3706" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Vertical Hyperbola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bacf7b85-1be6-512d-831b-0f1aa56dbbe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, \\frac{π}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156869} {:answer/id #uuid "bd839a41-3083-546a-b122-20649d4a0cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: t ∈ (-\\frac{π}{2}, 0]]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156870} {:answer/id #uuid "10ba4d52-6b2d-5756-bfb3-d04bac120305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: t ∈ (\\frac{π}{2}, π]]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156871} {:answer/id #uuid "225f65d0-8f4d-56cb-bd93-71e1177331f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: t ∈ [0, \\frac{π}{2})]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156872} {:answer/id #uuid "62f8a80f-777d-59f4-a2cf-655535777083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{π}{2})]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62f8a80f-777d-59f4-a2cf-655535777083" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[MATH: t ∈ [- π,-\\frac{π}{2})]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156873} :answer-field/id #uuid "34c635ea-502b-5d7d-ace0-86168b760590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186156868}] :question/id #uuid "41da8a73-410b-5efd-98c6-b09b7eda